    "reading": {
        "body": "Poland's mapped data center footprint consists of 21 facilities, with a pronounced concentration around Warsaw and a secondary cluster near Katowice and Kraków in the south.\n\nThe Warsaw pull is structural. Operators including Equinix, OVHcloud, Vantage Data Centers, Data4, Orange Polska, Atman, and NASK have all placed facilities within the metropolitan area or its immediate western fringe, around Jawczyce and Ożarów Mazowiecki. That density reflects proximity to fiber interconnection, national backbone infrastructure, and the power grid access that large facilities require. The southern cluster, centered on Katowice with the two PLAY facilities operated by 3S Data Center, and the two Polcom sites near Kraków, suggests a second zone of connectivity and power availability independent of the capital.\n\nThe data records names, operators, and coordinates for all 21 facilities but does not include capacity figures, power draw, planned expansions, or grid connection details. It is therefore a map of where compute infrastructure exists in Poland, not a measure of how much compute those buildings hold or how constrained their power supply is. Whether any of these facilities can absorb the load growth that AI workloads demand cannot be answered from this dataset alone.",
